Sure, here is a step-by-step guide to developing a Windows desktop application with multi-factor authentication, including facial recognition and a password backup option, using C# programming language:
1. Install Visual Studio: First, you need to install Visual Studio, which is an integrated development environment (IDE) for creating Windows desktop applications. You can download the latest version of Visual Studio from the official Microsoft website.
2. Create a new project: Open Visual Studio and create a new project by selecting "File" > "New" > "Project" and then selecting "Windows Forms App (.NET Framework)" from the list of templates. Give your project a name and click "Create".
3. Add the necessary NuGet packages: To use facial recognition in your application, you will need to add the following NuGet packages to your project: "Microsoft.Azure.CognitiveServices.Vision.Face" and "Microsoft.ProjectOxford.Face". You can add these packages by right-clicking on your project in the Solution Explorer and selecting "Manage NuGet Packages".
4. Set up the facial recognition functionality: Once you have added the necessary NuGet packages, you can set up the facial recognition functionality by creating a new class that handles the face detection and recognition logic. You can use the "FaceServiceClient" class provided by the "Microsoft.ProjectOxford.Face" package to detect and recognize faces. Here's an example code snippet:
```
using Microsoft.ProjectOxford.Face;
using Microsoft.ProjectOxford.Face.Contract;
public class FacialRecognition
{
    private readonly FaceServiceClient faceServiceClient;
    public FacialRecognition(string subscriptionKey, string endpoint)
    {
        faceServiceClient = new FaceServiceClient(subscriptionKey, endpoint);
    }
    public async Task<Guid?> DetectAndRecognizeFace(string imagePath, string personGroupId)
    {
        using (var stream = File.OpenRead(imagePath))
        {
            var faces = await faceServiceClient.DetectAsync(stream);
            var faceIds = faces.Select(face => face.FaceId).ToArray();
            var results = await faceServiceClient.IdentifyAsync(personGroupId, faceIds);
            var candidate = results.FirstOrDefault()?.Candidates.FirstOrDefault();
            return candidate?.PersonId;
        }
    }
}
```
This code creates a new class called "FacialRecognition" that takes a subscription key and endpoint as parameters and uses the "FaceServiceClient" class to detect and recognize faces in an image. You can call the "DetectAndRecognizeFace" method to detect and recognize faces in an image and get the ID of the recognized person.
5. Set up the password backup option: To set up the password backup option, you can use the built-in "System.Security.Cryptography" namespace in C# to encrypt and decrypt passwords. Here's an example code snippet:
```
using System.Security.Cryptography;
public class PasswordBackup
{
    private readonly byte[] key = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 };
    private readonly byte[] iv = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 };
    public string EncryptPassword(string password)
    {
        using (var aes = Aes.Create())
        {
            aes.Key = key;
            aes.IV = iv;
            var encryptor = aes.CreateEncryptor(aes.Key, aes.IV);
            using (var ms = new MemoryStream())
            {
                using (var cs = new CryptoStream(ms, encryptor, CryptoStreamMode.Write))
                {
                    using (var sw = new StreamWriter(cs))
                    {
                        sw.Write(password);
                    }
                    return Convert.ToBase64String(ms.ToArray());
                }
            }
        }
    }
    public string DecryptPassword(string encryptedPassword)
    {
        using (var aes = Aes.Create())
        {
            aes.Key = key;
            aes.IV = iv;
            var decryptor = aes.CreateDecryptor(aes.Key, aes.IV);
            using (var ms = new MemoryStream(Convert.FromBase64String(encryptedPassword)))
            {
                using (var cs = new CryptoStream(ms, decryptor, CryptoStreamMode.Read))
                {
                    using (var sr = new StreamReader(cs))
                    {
                        return sr.ReadToEnd();
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This code creates a new class called "PasswordBackup" that uses the "Aes" class to encrypt and decrypt passwords. You can call the "EncryptPassword" method to encrypt a password and the "DecryptPassword" method to decrypt a password.
