Here's a Java program that generates a list of 15 unique 5-digit codes that follow the given rules:
```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;
public class UniqueCodeGenerator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Set<String> codes = new HashSet<>();
        int count = 0;
        int num = 10000;

        while (count < 15) {
            String code = String.valueOf(num);
            int first = Integer.parseInt(String.valueOf(code.charAt(0)));
            int second = Integer.parseInt(String.valueOf(code.charAt(1)));
            int third = Integer.parseInt(String.valueOf(code.charAt(2)));
            int fourth = Integer.parseInt(String.valueOf(code.charAt(3)));
            int fifth = Integer.parseInt(String.valueOf(code.charAt(4)));

            if (isPrime(first) && isMultipleOfFour(second) && isPerfectSquare(third) && isPalindrome(fourth) && (second - fourth) == fifth) {
                codes.add(code);
                count++;
            }

            num++;
        }

        System.out.println(codes);
    }

    private static boolean isPrime(int n) {
        if (n < 2) {
            return false;
        }

        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(n); i++) {
            if (n % i == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    private static boolean isMultipleOfFour(int n) {
        return (n % 4) == 0;
    }

    private static boolean isPerfectSquare(int n) {
        int sqrt = (int) Math.sqrt(n);
        return (sqrt * sqrt) == n;
    }

    private static boolean isPalindrome(int n) {
        int reversed = 0;
        int original = n;

        while (n != 0) {
            int digit = n % 10;
            reversed = (reversed * 10) + digit;
            n /= 10;
        }

        return original == reversed;
    }
}
```
The program uses a `Set` to store the unique codes and a `while` loop to generate the codes. Each code is checked against the given rules using helper methods `isPrime`, `isMultipleOfFour`, `isPerfectSquare`, and `isPalindrome`. If a code satisfies all the rules, it is added to the set and the loop continues until 15 unique codes have been generated.
When the loop is finished, the program prints the set of codes.

