To optimize this code to have a time complexity of O(n), we can use a HashMap to keep track of the elements we have seen before. We can iterate through the ArrayList, and for each element, we check if it is already in the HashMap. If it is not, we add it to the HashMap and to a new ArrayList that will contain the unique elements. If it is already in the HashMap, we skip it.
Here's the optimized Java code:
```
ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<Integer>();
list.add(1);
list.add(2);
list.add(2);
list.add(3);
HashMap<Integer, Boolean> map = new HashMap<Integer, Boolean>();
ArrayList<Integer> uniqueList = new ArrayList<Integer>();
for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {
    Integer element = list.get(i);
    if (!map.containsKey(element)) {
        map.put(element, true);
        uniqueList.add(element);
    }
}
System.out.println(uniqueList);
