```java
import java.util.Scanner;
public class ReverseArray {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter the size of the array: ");
        int size = input.nextInt();
        int[] arr = new int[size];
        System.out.print("Enter the array elements: ");
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            arr[i] = input.nextInt();
        }
        System.out.print("Original array: ");
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            System.out.print(arr[i] + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();
        System.out.print("Reversed array: ");
        for (int i = size - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            System.out.print(arr[i] + " ");
        }
        input.close();
    }
}
```
Here's how it works:
1. First, we import the `Scanner` class to read input from the user.
2. We prompt the user to enter the size of the array and read it using `nextInt()`.
3. We create an integer array of the specified size using `new int[size]`.
4. We prompt the user to enter the array elements and read them one by one using a `for` loop.
5. We print the original array using another `for` loop.
6. We print the reversed array using a `for` loop that starts from the last element and goes backwards to the first element.
7. Finally, we close the `Scanner` object to free up resources.
