To analyze the grammatical structure, tense, and subordinate clauses of a given sentence using Java, you can use a natural language processing library like Stanford CoreNLP. Here is a sample Java code that can perform these linguistic analyses on a given sentence:
```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Properties;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreAnnotations;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreLabel;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreAnnotations.PartOfSpeechAnnotation;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreAnnotations.SentencesAnnotation;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreAnnotations.TokensAnnotation;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.Annotation;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.StanfordCoreNLP;
import edu.stanford.nlp.semgraph.SemanticGraph;
import edu.stanford.nlp.semgraph.SemanticGraphCoreAnnotations.CollapsedCCProcessedDependenciesAnnotation;
import edu.stanford.nlp.trees.Tree;
import edu.stanford.nlp.trees.TreeCoreAnnotations.TreeAnnotation;
import edu.stanford.nlp.util.CoreMap;
public class SentenceAnalyzer {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    // Set up Stanford CoreNLP pipeline
    Properties props = new Properties();
    props.setProperty("annotators", "tokenize, ssplit, pos, parse");
    StanfordCoreNLP pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP(props);
    // Define the input sentence
    String sentence = "Writer's block is a real thing.";
    // Create an annotation object
    Annotation annotation = new Annotation(sentence);
    // Process the annotation using the pipeline
    pipeline.annotate(annotation);
    // Get the list of sentences from the annotation
    List<CoreMap> sentences = annotation.get(SentencesAnnotation.class);
    // Loop through each sentence in the list
    for (CoreMap coreMap : sentences) {
      // Get the tree and dependencies for the sentence
      Tree tree = coreMap.get(TreeAnnotation.class);
      SemanticGraph dependencies = coreMap.get(CollapsedCCProcessedDependenciesAnnotation.class);
      // Get the subject and predicate of the sentence
      String subject = "";
      String predicate = "";
      for (CoreLabel token : coreMap.get(TokensAnnotation.class)) {
        String pos = token.get(PartOfSpeechAnnotation.class);
        if (pos.startsWith("N")) {
          subject += token.word() + " ";
        } else if (pos.startsWith("V")) {
          predicate += token.word() + " ";
        }
      }
      subject = subject.trim();
      predicate = predicate.trim();
      // Get the tense of the sentence
      String tense = tree.firstChild().label().value();
      // Get the subordinate clauses of the sentence
      List<Tree> clauses = tree.getLeaves();
      for (Tree clause : clauses) {
        if (clause.parent(tree).label().value().equals("SBAR")) {
          System.out.println("Subordinate clause: " + clause.toString());
        }
      }
      // Print the results
      System.out.println("Subject: " + subject);
      System.out.println("Predicate: " + predicate);
      System.out.println("Tense: " + tense);
      System.out.println("Dependencies: " + dependencies.toString());
    }
  }
}
```
In this code, we first set up the Stanford CoreNLP pipeline with the "tokenize", "ssplit", "pos", and "parse" annotators. Then, we define the input sentence and create an annotation object. We process the annotation using the pipeline and get the list of sentences from the annotation. We loop through each sentence in the list and get the tree and dependencies for the sentence. We then use the tree to get the subject and predicate of the sentence, the tense of the sentence, and the subordinate clauses of the sentence. Finally, we print the results.
Note that this is just a sample code and may not work for all sentences. You may need to modify the code to handle different sentence structures and grammatical rules.

